window.QUIZ_DATA = {"course": "Data Science Quiz", "topics": {"02": {"title": "Probability Foundations", "cards": [{"ID": "s2-01", "Topic": "2.1 · Random Variables", "Question": "What is a random variable?", "Short": "<p>A random variable is a measurable function that assigns a real number to each outcome of a random experiment. Measurability ensures that statements about its values correspond to events to which we can assign probabilities.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A random variable turns the outcome of an experiment into a numerical value. Formally, it is a measurable function from a sample space to the real numbers. I write this mapping as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F)\\to(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.1.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Here <span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb{P})\\)<\/span> is the probability space and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal B\\)<\/span> is the Borel sigma-algebra on the real numbers. Measurable means that the preimage of every Borel set is an event in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal F\\)<\/span>. In particular, for every real number <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, the set of outcomes for which <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\le x\\)<\/span> is an event, so its probability is defined.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"intuition\" class=\"unnumbered\">Intuition<\/h3>\n<p>The randomness is in the outcome <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\omega\\in\\Omega\\)<\/span>; once <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\omega\\)<\/span> is fixed, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X(\\omega)\\)<\/span> is just a deterministic number.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>Toss two coins. Let<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\Omega=\\{HH,HT,TH,TT\\}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.1.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and define <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)<\/span> as the number of heads. Then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X(HH)=2,\\qquad X(HT)=X(TH)=1,\\qquad X(TT)=0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.1.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Here <span class=\"math inline\">\\(H\\)<\/span> means <em>heads<\/em> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T\\)<\/span> means <em>tails<\/em>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.1.1)<\/strong> X is a measurable function from the sample space Omega, with sigma-algebra script F, to the real numbers, with the Borel sigma-algebra script B.<\/p><p><strong>(2.1.2)<\/strong> Omega is the set containing heads-heads, heads-tails, tails-heads, and tails-tails.<\/p><p><strong>(2.1.3)<\/strong> X of heads-heads equals two; X of heads-tails and X of tails-heads both equal one; X of tails-tails equals zero.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-02", "Topic": "2.2 · PMF, PDF, and CDF", "Question": "What are a PMF, a PDF, and a CDF?", "Short": "<p>A probability mass function gives the probability of each possible value of a discrete random variable. A probability density function is a nonnegative function whose integral over an interval gives the probability of falling in that interval. A cumulative distribution function gives the probability that a random variable is less than or equal to a specified value.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A probability mass function describes a discrete distribution by assigning a probability to each possible value. If I denote it by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_X\\)<\/span>, its definition is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[p_X(x)=\\mathbb{P}(X=x).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>It satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_X(x)\\ge0\\)<\/span> and<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\sum_x p_X(x)=1.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>A probability density function describes an absolutely continuous distribution through areas under a nonnegative curve. The density itself is not a point probability. Instead, integrating it over an interval gives the probability that the random variable falls in that interval, which I write as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(a&lt;X\\le b)=\\int_a^b f_X(x)\\,dx.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>It satisfies<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f_X(x)\\,dx=1.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a continuous random variable,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(X=x)=0\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>for every single point <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)<\/span>. A density value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_X(x)\\)<\/span> is therefore not itself a point probability, and a density may exceed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)<\/span> on a sufficiently short interval.<\/p>\n<p>A cumulative distribution function works for any random variable, including discrete and continuous ones. It accumulates probability up to a threshold x, including the threshold itself. Its definition is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[F_X(x)=\\mathbb{P}(X\\le x).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a continuous random variable with density,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[F_X(x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^x f_X(t)\\,dt,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Conversely, differentiating the CDF recovers its density almost everywhere; in particular, equality holds wherever the chosen density is continuous:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[f_X(x)=F&#39;_X(x).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.2.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>A continuous CDF need not have a density. In these notes, the continuous distribution families used for calculations are absolutely continuous.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.2.1)<\/strong> Lowercase p sub X of x equals the probability that capital X equals lowercase x.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.2)<\/strong> The sum of p sub X of x over all possible values of x equals one.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.3)<\/strong> The probability that X is greater than a and less than or equal to b equals the integral from a to b of f sub X of x, d x.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.4)<\/strong> The integral from negative infinity to infinity of f sub X of x, d x, equals one.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.5)<\/strong> The probability that X equals any particular value x is zero.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.6)<\/strong> Capital F sub X of x equals the probability that X is less than or equal to x.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.7)<\/strong> F sub X of x equals the integral from negative infinity to x of f sub X of t, d t.<\/p><p><strong>(2.2.8)<\/strong> The density f sub X of x equals the derivative of the CDF at x, almost everywhere.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-03", "Topic": "2.3 · Expectation, Moments, and Variance", "Question": "What are expectation, raw and central moments, variance, and standard deviation?", "Short": "<p>The expected value is the probability-weighted average of a random variable. Raw moments are expected powers of that variable, while central moments are expected powers of its deviation from its mean. Variance is the second central moment: the expected squared deviation from the mean. Standard deviation is the square root of variance.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The expected value describes the center of a distribution by weighting possible values according to their probabilities. For an integrable random variable, its general definition is an integral with respect to the probability measure:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\int_{\\Omega}X(\\omega)\\,d\\mathbb{P}(\\omega).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a discrete random variable,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\sum_x x\\mathbb{P}(X=x),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and for a continuous random variable with density,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x f_X(x)\\,dx.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This probability-weighted center is also the first raw moment. Moments extend the same averaging idea to powers of a random variable.<\/p>\n<p>The k-th raw moment averages the k-th power of X, so I define it by<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[m_k=\\mathbb{E}[X^k],\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)<\/span>-th central moment is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mu_k=\\mathbb{E}[(X-\\mathbb{E}[X])^k].\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Therefore<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[m_1=\\mathbb{E}[X].\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Variance measures spread around the expected value. It is the second central moment, so the entire deviation is squared before taking the expectation:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\mathbb{E}[(X-\\mathbb{E}[X])^2].}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Expanding the square gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\mathbb{E}[X^2]-\\mathbb{E}[X]^2.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The standard deviation is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{SD}(X)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.9)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>These definitions explain how shifting or rescaling a random variable changes its mean and spread. For constants a and b, expectation transforms as follows:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[aX+b]=a\\mathbb{E}[X]+b,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.10)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\\operatorname{Var}(X).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.11)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>More generally, expectation is linear:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^n a_iX_i\\right] =\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i\\mathbb{E}[X_i],\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.3.12)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>without any independence assumption.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.3.1)<\/strong> The expected value of X is the integral over Omega of X of omega with respect to the probability measure P.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.2)<\/strong> The expected value of X is the sum, over all possible values x, of x times the probability that X equals x.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.3)<\/strong> The expected value of X is the integral from negative infinity to infinity of x times f sub X of x, d x.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.4)<\/strong> The k-th raw moment of X is the expected value of X to the power of k.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.5)<\/strong> The k-th central moment of X is the expected value of the quantity X minus its expected value, raised to the power of k.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.6)<\/strong> M sub one equals the expected value of X.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.7)<\/strong> The variance of X is the expected value of the squared difference between X and its mean.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.8)<\/strong> The variance of X equals the expected value of X squared minus the square of the expected value of X.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.9)<\/strong> The standard deviation of X is the square root of its variance.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.10)<\/strong> The expected value of a times X plus b equals a times the expected value of X plus b.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.11)<\/strong> The variance of a times X plus b equals a squared times the variance of X.<\/p><p><strong>(2.3.12)<\/strong> The expected value of the sum, from i equals one to n, of a sub i times X sub i equals the sum, over the same indices, of a sub i times the expected value of X sub i.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-04", "Topic": "2.5 · Conditional Probability", "Question": "What is conditional probability?", "Short": "<p>Conditional probability is the probability of an event after restricting attention to outcomes where another event has occurred.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Conditional probability renormalizes probabilities within the conditioning event. If B has positive probability, the probability of A given B is the probability of their intersection divided by the probability of B:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid B)=\\frac{\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)}{\\mathbb{P}(B)}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.5.1)<\/div><\/div>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.5.1)<\/strong> The probability of A given B equals the probability of A and B divided by the probability of B, provided that the probability of B is positive.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-05", "Topic": "2.5 · Bayes' Theorem", "Question": "What is Bayes’ theorem?", "Short": "<p>Bayes’ theorem expresses the probability of A given B in terms of the probability of B given A and the marginal probabilities of A and B. It lets us update a prior probability after observing evidence.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Bayes’ theorem describes how to update the probability of an event A after observing an event B. The prior probability of A is multiplied by the probability of observing B given A, then divided by the overall probability of B. Assuming <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{P}(A)&gt;0\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{P}(B)&gt;0\\)<\/span>, I write this as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid B)=\\frac{\\mathbb{P}(B\\mid A)\\mathbb{P}(A)}{\\mathbb{P}(B)}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.5.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"derivation\" class=\"unnumbered\">Derivation<\/h3>\n<p>Bayes’ theorem follows by rewriting the numerator using the definition of conditional probability in the opposite direction. The same joint probability has two equivalent expressions:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)=\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid B)\\mathbb{P}(B)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.5.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)=\\mathbb{P}(B\\mid A)\\mathbb{P}(A).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.5.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Equating these two expressions and dividing by the probability of B gives Bayes’ theorem.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>I can also expand the denominator using the law of total probability. If the events <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_1,\\dots,A_k\\)<\/span> partition the sample space and the conditional probabilities used are defined, then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(B)=\\sum_{j=1}^k\\mathbb{P}(B\\mid A_j)\\mathbb{P}(A_j),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.5.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>so<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(A_i\\mid B)= \\frac{\\mathbb{P}(B\\mid A_i)\\mathbb{P}(A_i)} {\\sum_{j=1}^k\\mathbb{P}(B\\mid A_j)\\mathbb{P}(A_j)}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.5.6)<\/div><\/div>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.5.4)<\/strong> The probability of A given B equals the probability of B given A times the probability of A, all divided by the probability of B.<\/p><p><strong>(2.5.2)<\/strong> The probability of A and B equals the probability of A given B times the probability of B.<\/p><p><strong>(2.5.3)<\/strong> The probability of both A and B can be written as the probability of A given B times the probability of B, or as the probability of B given A times the probability of A.<\/p><p><strong>(2.5.5)<\/strong> The probability of B is the sum, from j equals one to k, of the probability of B given A sub j times the probability of A sub j.<\/p><p><strong>(2.5.6)<\/strong> The probability of A sub i given B equals the probability of B given A sub i times the probability of A sub i, divided by the sum, from j equals one to k, of the probability of B given A sub j times the probability of A sub j.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-06", "Topic": "2.6 · Independent Events", "Question": "What are independent events?", "Short": "<p>Independent events are events whose joint probability equals the product of their individual probabilities. When the conditioning event has positive probability, knowing that it occurred does not change the probability of the other event.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Independence means that the joint probability factorizes into the product of the two marginal probabilities. This gives a definition that also works for zero-probability events:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)=\\mathbb{P}(A)\\mathbb{P}(B).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.6.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{P}(B)&gt;0\\)<\/span>, this is equivalent to<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(A\\mid B)=\\mathbb{P}(A).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.6.2)<\/div><\/div>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.6.1)<\/strong> A and B are independent if the probability of both A and B equals the probability of A times the probability of B.<\/p><p><strong>(2.6.2)<\/strong> The probability of A given B equals the probability of A.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-07", "Topic": "2.6 · Mutually Exclusive Events", "Question": "What are mutually exclusive events?", "Short": "<p>Mutually exclusive, or disjoint, events are events that cannot occur together: they have no outcomes in common.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Disjointness concerns which outcomes the events contain, rather than how their probabilities factorize. Events A and B are disjoint if their intersection is empty:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{A\\cap B=\\varnothing.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.6.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Then necessarily<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)=0.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.6.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The converse need not hold in a general probability space because nonempty events can have probability zero.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"follow-up\" class=\"unnumbered\">Follow-up<\/h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\)<\/span> are disjoint and both have positive probability, they cannot be independent, because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{P}(A\\cap B)=0\\)<\/span> whereas <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{P}(A)\\mathbb{P}(B)&gt;0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.6.3)<\/strong> A intersection B is the empty set. In words, A and B have no outcomes in common.<\/p><p><strong>(2.6.4)<\/strong> The probability of A intersection B is zero.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-08", "Topic": "2.7 · Covariance and Correlation", "Question": "What are covariance and correlation?", "Short": "<p>Covariance is the expected product of two random variables’ deviations from their respective expected values. Pearson correlation is covariance divided by the product of their standard deviations. Correlation measures linear association on a scale from minus one to one.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Covariance measures whether two variables tend to deviate from their expected values in the same or opposite directions. Assuming finite second moments, I calculate it as the expected product of those deviations:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=\\mathbb{E}[(X-\\mathbb{E}[X])(Y-\\mathbb{E}[Y])] =\\mathbb{E}[XY]-\\mathbb{E}[X]\\mathbb{E}[Y]. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.7.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Covariance measures linear co-movement but depends on scale. Its physical units are the product of the units of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)<\/span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Pearson correlation removes the dependence on measurement units by normalizing covariance. Specifically, I divide by the product of the standard deviations:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\rho_{X,Y}=\\operatorname{Corr}(X,Y) =\\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y}, }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.7.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>provided <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma_X,\\sigma_Y&gt;0\\)<\/span>. It is dimensionless and satisfies<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[-1\\le\\rho_{X,Y}\\le1.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.7.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"follow-up\" class=\"unnumbered\">Follow-up<\/h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y\\)<\/span> and the required moments exist, then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=0\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.7.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and therefore <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Corr}(X,Y)=0\\)<\/span> when both variances are positive. The converse is false in general.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\sim\\operatorname{Uniform}(-1,1)\\)<\/span> and define <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=X^2\\)<\/span>. Then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y\\)<\/span> is completely determined by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)<\/span>, so the variables are dependent. However,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=\\mathbb{E}[X^3]-\\mathbb{E}[X]\\mathbb{E}[X^2]=0\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.7.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>by symmetry. Thus zero correlation only excludes linear association, not general dependence.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"follow-up-1\" class=\"unnumbered\">Follow-up<\/h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\((X,Y)\\)<\/span> is jointly Gaussian, zero covariance is sufficient for independence.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.7.1)<\/strong> The covariance of X and Y is the expected product of their deviations from their respective means. Equivalently, it is the expected value of X times Y minus the product of their expected values.<\/p><p><strong>(2.7.2)<\/strong> The correlation of X and Y is their covariance divided by the product of their standard deviations.<\/p><p><strong>(2.7.3)<\/strong> Rho sub X Y lies between minus one and one, inclusive.<\/p><p><strong>(2.7.4)<\/strong> The covariance of X and Y equals zero.<\/p><p><strong>(2.7.5)<\/strong> The covariance of X and Y equals the expected value of X cubed minus the expected value of X times the expected value of X squared. By symmetry, this equals zero.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-09", "Topic": "2.8 · Law of Total Expectation and Law of Total Variance", "Question": "What do the laws of total expectation and total variance state?", "Short": "<p>The law of total expectation says that averaging a conditional expectation gives the unconditional expectation. The law of total variance separates overall variance into expected variance within groups and variance between group means.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The law of total expectation lets me calculate a mean in two stages: first condition on Y, then average the resulting conditional means over Y. For an integrable X, this is written as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\mathbb{E}[\\mathbb{E}[X\\mid Y]].}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.8.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The law of total variance applies the same conditioning idea to spread. For X with a finite second moment, I add the expected conditional variance to the variance of the conditional mean:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\mathbb{E}[\\operatorname{Var}(X\\mid Y)]+\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb{E}[X\\mid Y]). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.8.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The first term captures variation that remains within a group defined by Y; the second captures variation in average outcomes across those groups. For example, if Y identifies a customer segment, overall spending variance includes both variation within segments and differences in their average spending.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.8.1)<\/strong> The expected value of X equals the expected value of the conditional expectation of X given Y.<\/p><p><strong>(2.8.2)<\/strong> The variance of X equals the expected conditional variance of X given Y, plus the variance of the conditional expectation of X given Y.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-10", "Topic": "2.9 · Law of Large Numbers", "Question": "What is the law of large numbers?", "Short": "<p>The law of large numbers says that the sample mean converges to the population mean as the sample size grows. In the weak law for independent and identically distributed observations with a finite mean, this is convergence in probability.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The weak law of large numbers describes where the sample mean goes as the sample grows. For iid observations with a finite mean mu, the result is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\bar X_n=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nX_i\\xrightarrow{P}\\mu. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.9.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Thus the sample mean is a consistent estimator of the population mean.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.9.2)<\/strong> X bar sub n is one over n times the sum of X sub i from i equals one to n. It converges in probability to mu.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}, {"ID": "s2-11", "Topic": "2.9 · Central Limit Theorem", "Question": "What is the central limit theorem?", "Short": "<p>The central limit theorem says that, for independent and identically distributed observations with positive finite variance, the centered and properly scaled sample mean converges in distribution to a standard normal random variable.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>The central limit theorem describes the shape and scale of fluctuations around that mean. For independent and identically distributed observations, I assume<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[X_i]=\\mu,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(X_i)=\\sigma^2\\in(0,\\infty),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.9.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i-n\\mu}{\\sigma\\sqrt n} \\xrightarrow{d} \\mathcal{N}(0,1). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.9.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Equivalently,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\frac{\\sqrt n(\\bar X_n-\\mu)}{\\sigma}\\xrightarrow{d}\\mathcal{N}(0,1). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.9.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For large <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)<\/span> this motivates the approximation<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\bar X_n\\approx\\mathcal{N}\\left(\\mu,\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.9.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The convergence used here is convergence in distribution. It means that the CDFs converge to the limiting CDF:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[F_{Y_n}(x)\\to F_Y(x)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(2.9.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>at every continuity point of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F_Y\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"follow-up\" class=\"unnumbered\">Follow-up<\/h3>\n<p>The LLN concerns concentration around the mean; the CLT concerns the distribution of scaled estimation error. The CLT explains why approximately Gaussian sampling distributions appear throughout estimation, confidence intervals, hypothesis testing, and A/B experiments even when individual observations are not Gaussian.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(2.9.3)<\/strong> Each X sub i has mean mu and variance sigma squared, which is strictly positive and finite.<\/p><p><strong>(2.9.4)<\/strong> The sum of X sub i from i equals one to n, minus n times mu, all divided by sigma times the square root of n, converges in distribution to a standard normal random variable.<\/p><p><strong>(2.9.5)<\/strong> The square root of n times the quantity X bar sub n minus mu, all divided by sigma, converges in distribution to a standard normal random variable.<\/p><p><strong>(2.9.6)<\/strong> For large n, X bar sub n is approximately normally distributed with mean mu and variance sigma squared divided by n.<\/p><p><strong>(2.9.7)<\/strong> The CDF of Y sub n, evaluated at x, converges to the CDF of Y, evaluated at x, at every continuity point of the limiting CDF.<\/p>", "Deck": "02"}]}, "03": {"title": "Probability Distributions", "cards": [{"ID": "s3-01", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.1 · Bernoulli Distribution", "Question": "What is a Bernoulli distribution?", "Short": "<p>A random variable follows a Bernoulli distribution with parameter p if it takes the value one with probability p and zero with probability one minus p. It models one binary outcome; its mean is p and its variance is p times one minus p.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A random variable follows a Bernoulli distribution when it takes only the values zero and one, with probabilities one minus p and p, respectively. To connect that definition to an experiment, I call its two outcomes failure and success. Such an experiment is a Bernoulli trial, and its numerical outcome is the indicator<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X=\\begin{cases} 1,&amp;\\text{success},\\\\ 0,&amp;\\text{failure}. \\end{cases}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>I denote the resulting distribution by<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{X\\sim\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p)},\\qquad 0\\le p\\le1.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Its support is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}\\)<\/span> and its PMF is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(X=x)=p^x(1-p)^{1-x},\\qquad x\\in\\{0,1\\}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The mean is the probability of success, because only the value one contributes to the expectation. The mean and variance are<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[X]=p,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=p(1-p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Indeed, since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X^2=X\\)<\/span> for a Bernoulli variable,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\mathbb{E}[X^2]-\\mathbb{E}[X]^2=p-p^2=p(1-p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>Click/no click, default/no default, fraud/no fraud, and conversion/no conversion are natural Bernoulli outcomes.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>For iid <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_1,\\dots,X_n\\sim\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p)\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[X]=p.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Equating the theoretical first moment with the sample first moment gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat p_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=\\bar X.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>To derive the maximum likelihood estimator, I treat the observations as fixed and multiply their Bernoulli probabilities under independence:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(p)=\\prod_{i=1}^n p^{x_i}(1-p)^{1-x_i}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.9)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S=\\sum_i x_i\\)<\/span>. Then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(p)=p^S(1-p)^{n-S},\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.10)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell(p)=S\\log p+(n-S)\\log(1-p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.11)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Differentiating,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell&#39;(p)=\\frac{S}{p}-\\frac{n-S}{1-p}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.12)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For an interior maximum, I set this derivative equal to zero and solve for p. This gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[S(1-p)=p(n-S)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad S=np,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.13)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>so<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat p_{\\mathrm{MLE}}=\\frac Sn=\\bar X.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.1.14)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0&lt;S&lt;n\\)<\/span> the derivative gives an interior maximum. If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S=0\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S=n\\)<\/span>, maximize on the boundary to obtain <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\hat p=0\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)<\/span>, respectively. Thus the MoM and MLE estimates coincide.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.1.1)<\/strong> X equals one for success and zero for failure.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.2)<\/strong> X follows a Bernoulli distribution with success probability p, where p is between zero and one, inclusive.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.3)<\/strong> The probability that X equals x is p to the power of x times the quantity one minus p raised to the power of one minus x, for x equal to zero or one.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.4)<\/strong> The expected value of X equals p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.5)<\/strong> The expected value of X is p, and the variance of X is p times one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.6)<\/strong> The variance of X equals the expected value of X squared minus the square of its expected value. For Bernoulli X, this becomes p minus p squared, or p times one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.7)<\/strong> The first theoretical moment is the expected value of X, which equals p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.8)<\/strong> The method-of-moments estimate p hat equals X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.9)<\/strong> L of p is the product, from i equals one to n, of p to the power x sub i times the quantity one minus p raised to the power one minus x sub i.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.10)<\/strong> L of p equals p to the power S times the quantity one minus p raised to the power n minus S.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.11)<\/strong> Ell of p equals S times log p plus the quantity n minus S times log of one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.12)<\/strong> Ell prime of p equals S divided by p, minus the quantity n minus S divided by the quantity one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.13)<\/strong> S times one minus p equals p times the quantity n minus S. Rearranging gives S equals n times p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.1.14)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate p hat equals S divided by n, which is X bar: the observed success rate.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-02", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.2 · Binomial Distribution", "Question": "What is a binomial distribution?", "Short": "<p>A random variable follows a binomial distribution with parameters n and p if it counts successes in n independent Bernoulli trials with the same success probability p. Its possible values are zero through n, its mean is n times p, and its variance is n times p times one minus p.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A binomial distribution describes the number of successes in a fixed number of independent trials with the same probability of success. I construct the count from iid Bernoulli variables:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X_1,\\dots,X_n\\overset{\\mathrm{iid}}{\\sim}\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The number of successes<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[S_n=\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>has a binomial distribution:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{S_n\\sim\\operatorname{Binomial}(n,p).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The count takes values from zero to n. To obtain exactly k successes, I choose their positions in n choose k ways and multiply by the probability of any one such arrangement. Therefore its probability mass function is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(S_n=k)=\\binom nk p^k(1-p)^{n-k}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Linearity of expectation gives the mean, and independence lets me add the variances of the individual trials. Consequently,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[S_n]=np,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\operatorname{Var}(S_n)=np(1-p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"follow-up\" class=\"unnumbered\">Follow-up<\/h3>\n<p>The binomial construction requires a fixed number <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)<\/span> of trials, binary outcomes, a common success probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\)<\/span>, and independence across trials. These assumptions are part of the construction rather than a separate definition.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(10{,}000\\)<\/span> independently exposed users each convert with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\)<\/span>, the total number of conversions is approximately or exactly binomial depending on the experimental design assumptions.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>For estimation, suppose I observe m independent binomial counts, each based on n trials, where n is known. The theoretical first moment is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[Y]=np,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>we obtain<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat p_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=\\frac{\\bar Y}{n}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>To estimate p by maximum likelihood, I multiply the probability masses for those observed counts. Terms that do not depend on p can be omitted, giving<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(p)\\propto\\prod_{j=1}^m p^{y_j}(1-p)^{n-y_j}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.9)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Ignoring factors independent of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell(p)=\\left(\\sum_jy_j\\right)\\log p+ \\left(mn-\\sum_jy_j\\right)\\log(1-p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.10)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Differentiating and setting to zero gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat p_{\\mathrm{MLE}} =\\frac{\\sum_jy_j}{mn} =\\frac{\\bar Y}{n}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.2.11)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>If all trials fail or all succeed, the maximum is at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p=0\\)<\/span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)<\/span>; the same formula applies. Again MLE and MoM coincide.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.2.1)<\/strong> X sub one through X sub n are independent and identically distributed Bernoulli random variables with success probability p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.2)<\/strong> S sub n equals the sum of X sub i from i equals one to n.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.3)<\/strong> S sub n follows a binomial distribution with n trials and success probability p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.4)<\/strong> The probability that S sub n equals k is n choose k times p to the power of k times the quantity one minus p raised to the power of n minus k.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.5)<\/strong> The expected value of S sub n equals n times p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.6)<\/strong> The expected number of successes is n times p, and the variance is n times p times one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.7)<\/strong> The expected value of Y equals n times p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.8)<\/strong> P hat equals Y bar divided by n.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.9)<\/strong> The likelihood is proportional to the product, from j equals one to m, of p to the power y sub j times the quantity one minus p raised to the power n minus y sub j.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.10)<\/strong> The log-likelihood is the sum of the observed counts times log p, plus the quantity m times n minus that sum, multiplied by log of one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.2.11)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate of p is the total number of successes divided by m times n, or Y bar divided by n.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-03", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.3 · Geometric Distribution", "Question": "What is a geometric distribution?", "Short": "<p>A random variable follows a geometric distribution with parameter p if it counts the trials up to and including the first success in independent Bernoulli trials with constant success probability p. Under this convention it takes values one, two, and so on, and its mean is one over p.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A geometric distribution describes how many independent Bernoulli trials are needed to obtain the first success, including the successful trial. I use a fixed success probability p greater than zero. Starting from<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X_1,X_2,\\dots\\overset{\\mathrm{iid}}{\\sim}\\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p)\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>I define the trial number of the first success as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[T=\\min\\{k\\ge1:X_k=1\\}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Then, for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0&lt;p\\le1\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{T\\sim\\operatorname{Geometric}(p)}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Its possible values are the positive integers. For the first success to occur at trial k, the preceding k minus one trials must fail and trial k must succeed. Independence therefore gives the probability mass function<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(T=k)=(1-p)^{k-1}p. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The mean and variance are<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}[T]=\\frac1p,} \\qquad \\boxed{\\operatorname{Var}(T)=\\frac{1-p}{p^2}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>Another convention defines a geometric random variable as the number of failures before the first success, with support <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1,2,\\dots\\}\\)<\/span> and mean <span class=\"math inline\">\\((1-p)/p\\)<\/span>. State the convention explicitly.<\/p>\n<p>The geometric distribution also has the memoryless property: after a run of failures, the distribution of the remaining number of trials until success is unchanged. Formally,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(T&gt;s+t\\mid T&gt;s)=\\mathbb{P}(T&gt;t). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For the chosen convention,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(T&gt;k)=(1-p)^k,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>which proves the property for nonnegative integers <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s,t\\)<\/span> with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{P}(T&gt;s)&gt;0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{E}[T]=1/p\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\bar T=\\frac1p \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\boxed{\\hat p_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=\\frac1{\\bar T}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>For independent observed waiting times, I form the likelihood by multiplying their probability masses:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(p)=\\prod_{i=1}^n(1-p)^{t_i-1}p =p^n(1-p)^{\\sum_i t_i-n}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.9)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Hence<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell(p)=n\\log p+(\\sum_i t_i-n)\\log(1-p).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.10)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Differentiating,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell&#39;(p)=\\frac np- \\frac{\\sum_i t_i-n}{1-p}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.11)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Setting this equal to zero yields<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[n(1-p)=p(\\sum_i t_i-n) \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad n=p\\sum_i t_i,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.12)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>so<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat p_{\\mathrm{MLE}}=\\frac{n}{\\sum_i t_i}=\\frac1{\\bar T}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.3.13)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification-1\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>If all observed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t_i=1\\)<\/span>, the geometric MLE is the boundary value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p=1\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.3.1)<\/strong> X sub one, X sub two, and so on are independent and identically distributed Bernoulli variables with success probability p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.3.2)<\/strong> T is the smallest integer k greater than or equal to one for which X sub k equals one: the trial number of the first success.<\/p><p><strong>(3.3.3)<\/strong> T follows a geometric distribution with success probability p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.3.4)<\/strong> The probability that T equals k is the quantity one minus p raised to the power of k minus one, times p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.3.5)<\/strong> The expected value of T is one over p. Its variance is one minus p, all divided by p squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.3.6)<\/strong> The probability that T exceeds s plus t, given that T exceeds s, equals the probability that T exceeds t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.3.7)<\/strong> The probability that T exceeds k is the quantity one minus p raised to the power of k.<\/p><p><strong>(3.3.8)<\/strong> P hat equals one divided by T bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.3.9)<\/strong> The likelihood is the product of one minus p raised to the power t sub i minus one, times p. Collecting the powers gives p to the power n times one minus p raised to the sum of t sub i minus n.<\/p><p><strong>(3.3.10)<\/strong> Ell of p is n times log p plus the quantity sum of t sub i minus n, multiplied by log of one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.3.11)<\/strong> Ell prime of p equals n over p minus the quantity sum of t sub i minus n, divided by one minus p.<\/p><p><strong>(3.3.12)<\/strong> N times one minus p equals p times the quantity sum of t sub i minus n. Rearranging gives n equals p times the sum of t sub i.<\/p><p><strong>(3.3.13)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate of p is n divided by the sum of the observed waiting times, or one divided by T bar.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-04", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.4 · Poisson Distribution", "Question": "What is a Poisson distribution?", "Short": "<p>A Poisson distribution is a discrete distribution over nonnegative integer counts with parameter lambda. The probability of count k is e to the negative lambda times lambda to the power of k, divided by k factorial. Its mean and variance both equal lambda.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A random variable follows a Poisson distribution with positive parameter lambda if it takes nonnegative integer values with the following probability mass function:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(X=k)=e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^k}{k!}, \\qquad k=0,1,2,\\dots }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>and we write<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X\\sim\\operatorname{Poisson}(\\lambda).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The parameter lambda determines both the center and spread of the count distribution. Its defining moment relationship is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\lambda,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\lambda.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"intuition\" class=\"unnumbered\">Intuition<\/h3>\n<p><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span> is the expected number of events in the chosen observation window. If an event process has physical rate <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\)<\/span> events per unit time and the window has length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t\\)<\/span>, then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\lambda=rt.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Here <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\)<\/span> has units <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/\\text{time}\\)<\/span>, while <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)<\/span> is an expected count and therefore dimensionless as a pure number.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"example\" class=\"unnumbered\">Example<\/h3>\n<p>If calls arrive at an average rate of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r=12\\)<\/span> per hour, then the expected count in half an hour is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda=12\\cdot0.5=6\\)<\/span> and a Poisson model would write <span class=\"math inline\">\\(N(0.5)\\sim\\operatorname{Poisson}(6)\\)<\/span> under suitable assumptions.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<h4 id=\"poisson-limit-theorem.\">Poisson limit theorem.<\/h4>\n<p>The Poisson distribution also arises as a rare-event limit of binomial counts. To explain this, I start with<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X_n\\sim\\operatorname{Binomial}(n,p_n).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>If<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ n\\to\\infty,\\qquad p_n\\to0,\\qquad np_n\\to\\lambda\\in(0,\\infty), }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>then<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{X_n\\xrightarrow{d}\\operatorname{Poisson}(\\lambda).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For each fixed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\mathbb{P}(X_n=k) =e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^k}{k!}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>A canonical scaling is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_n=\\lambda/n\\)<\/span> for sufficiently large <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)<\/span>: each individual opportunity becomes rarer while the number of opportunities grows so that the expected count <span class=\"math inline\">\\(np_n\\)<\/span> remains finite.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\lambda\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\lambda_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=\\bar X.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.9)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>To estimate lambda from iid counts, I write the likelihood as the product of their Poisson probability masses:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(\\lambda)=\\prod_{i=1}^n e^{-\\lambda}\\frac{\\lambda^{x_i}}{x_i!}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.10)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Thus<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell(\\lambda) =-n\\lambda+\\left(\\sum_i x_i\\right)\\log\\lambda- \\sum_i\\log(x_i!).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.11)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Differentiating,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell&#39;(\\lambda)=-n+\\frac{\\sum_i x_i}{\\lambda}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.12)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Therefore<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\lambda_{\\mathrm{MLE}}=\\bar X.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.4.13)<\/div><\/div>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>With the stated parameter space <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda&gt;0\\)<\/span>, an all-zero sample has no attained MLE: the likelihood approaches its supremum as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\downarrow0\\)<\/span>. If the model includes the degenerate distribution at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda=0\\)<\/span>, the MLE is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\bar X=0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.4.1)<\/strong> The probability that X equals k is e to the power of negative lambda, times lambda to the power of k, divided by k factorial. K can be any nonnegative integer.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.2)<\/strong> X follows a Poisson distribution with parameter lambda.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.3)<\/strong> The expected value and the variance of X both equal lambda.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.4)<\/strong> Lambda equals the event rate r times the duration t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.5)<\/strong> X sub n follows a binomial distribution with n trials and success probability p sub n.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.6)<\/strong> N tends to infinity, p sub n tends to zero, and n times p sub n tends to a positive finite value lambda.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.7)<\/strong> X sub n converges in distribution to a Poisson random variable with parameter lambda.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.8)<\/strong> As n tends to infinity, the probability that X sub n equals k tends to e to the power negative lambda times lambda to the power k, divided by k factorial.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.9)<\/strong> Lambda hat equals X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.10)<\/strong> L of lambda is the product, from i equals one to n, of e to the power negative lambda times lambda to the power x sub i, divided by x sub i factorial.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.11)<\/strong> Ell of lambda equals negative n times lambda, plus the sum of x sub i times log lambda, minus the sum of log of x sub i factorial.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.12)<\/strong> Ell prime of lambda equals negative n plus the sum of the observations divided by lambda.<\/p><p><strong>(3.4.13)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate of lambda equals the sample mean X bar.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-05", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.5 · Poisson Process and Exponential Waiting Times", "Question": "What is a Poisson process, and how is it related to exponential waiting times?", "Short": "<p>A homogeneous Poisson process is a counting process that starts at zero, has independent increments, and has Poisson-distributed counts over each interval with mean equal to a constant rate times the interval length. Its inter-arrival waiting times are independent exponential variables with that rate.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A homogeneous Poisson process describes events accumulating over time at a constant rate r. The count starts at zero, counts in disjoint intervals are independent, and an interval count depends on the interval only through its length. The distribution of an increment is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ N(t)-N(s)\\sim\\operatorname{Poisson}(r(t-s)),\\qquad 0\\le s&lt;t. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.5.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Thus the expected number of events in an interval of length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(h\\)<\/span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(rh\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>Keeping <span class=\"math inline\">\\(N(0)=0\\)<\/span> and stationary independent increments, an equivalent infinitesimal description is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(N(t+h)-N(t)=1)=rh+o(h),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.5.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(N(t+h)-N(t)\\ge2)=o(h),\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.5.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(h\\downarrow0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>The connection to exponential waiting times follows by considering the first event. If I call its waiting time T sub one, waiting longer than t means that no event has occurred by time t. Thus<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{P}(T_1&gt;t)=\\mathbb{P}(N(t)=0)=e^{-rt},\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.5.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>so<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{T_1\\sim\\operatorname{Exponential}(r).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.5.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Thus Poisson describes <em>how many<\/em> events occur in an interval, whereas the exponential distribution describes <em>how long<\/em> one waits for the next event.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.5.1)<\/strong> N of t minus N of s follows a Poisson distribution with parameter r times the quantity t minus s, for zero less than or equal to s and s less than t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.5.2)<\/strong> The probability that the increment from time t to time t plus h equals one is r times h plus little-oh of h.<\/p><p><strong>(3.5.3)<\/strong> Over a short interval of length h, the probability of one event is r times h plus little-oh of h, and the probability of two or more events is little-oh of h. Little-oh of h means a remainder that, divided by h, tends to zero as h tends to zero.<\/p><p><strong>(3.5.4)<\/strong> The probability that the first waiting time exceeds t equals the probability of zero events by time t, which is e to the power of negative r times t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.5.5)<\/strong> T sub one follows an exponential distribution with rate r.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-06", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.6 · Exponential Distribution", "Question": "What is an exponential distribution?", "Short": "<p>An exponential distribution is a continuous distribution on nonnegative values with rate parameter lambda and density lambda times e raised to the power of negative lambda times t. It models the waiting time to the next event in a homogeneous Poisson process; its mean is one over lambda.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A random variable follows an exponential distribution with positive rate lambda if it is nonnegative and has the following density:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ f_T(t)=\\lambda e^{-\\lambda t},\\qquad t\\ge0. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Integrating this density from zero to t gives the CDF. Subtracting the CDF from one gives the survival probability, which is the probability of waiting longer than t. For nonnegative t, these are<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[F_T(t)=1-e^{-\\lambda t}, \\qquad \\mathbb{P}(T&gt;t)=e^{-\\lambda t}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The mean and variance are<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}[T]=\\frac1\\lambda,} \\qquad \\boxed{\\operatorname{Var}(T)=\\frac1{\\lambda^2}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The exponential distribution is memoryless. If no event has occurred during an initial waiting period, the distribution of the additional waiting time is the same as it was at the start. This property is expressed by<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\mathbb{P}(T&gt;s+t\\mid T&gt;s)=\\mathbb{P}(T&gt;t). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Here memorylessness holds for real <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s,t\\ge0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{E}[T]=1/\\lambda\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\lambda_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=\\frac1{\\bar T}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>To estimate the rate from independent waiting times, I multiply the exponential densities:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(\\lambda)=\\prod_i\\lambda e^{-\\lambda t_i} =\\lambda^n e^{-\\lambda\\sum_i t_i}.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Hence<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell(\\lambda)=n\\log\\lambda-\\lambda\\sum_i t_i,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell&#39;(\\lambda)=\\frac n\\lambda-\\sum_i t_i.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sum_i t_i&gt;0\\)<\/span> (which holds almost surely), setting this to zero yields<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat\\lambda_{\\mathrm{MLE}} =\\frac n{\\sum_i t_i} =\\frac1{\\bar T}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.6.9)<\/div><\/div>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.6.1)<\/strong> Lowercase f sub T of t equals lambda times e raised to the power of negative lambda times t, for t greater than or equal to zero. The exponent is the negative of the product lambda times t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.2)<\/strong> The CDF at t is one minus e to the power of negative lambda times t. The probability that T exceeds t is e to the power of negative lambda times t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.3)<\/strong> The expected waiting time is one over lambda, and its variance is one over lambda squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.4)<\/strong> The probability that T exceeds s plus t, given that T exceeds s, equals the probability that T exceeds t.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.5)<\/strong> Lambda hat equals one divided by T bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.6)<\/strong> The likelihood is the product of the exponential densities. It equals lambda to the power n times e raised to the negative of lambda times the sum of the observed waiting times.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.7)<\/strong> Ell of lambda equals n times log lambda minus lambda times the sum of the observed waiting times.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.8)<\/strong> Ell prime of lambda equals n over lambda minus the sum of the observed waiting times.<\/p><p><strong>(3.6.9)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate of lambda is n divided by the sum of the observed waiting times, or the reciprocal of their sample mean.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-07", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.7 · Normal Distribution", "Question": "What is a normal distribution?", "Short": "<p>A normal distribution is a continuous distribution on the real line with a symmetric bell-shaped density, determined by its mean mu and positive variance sigma squared. Subtracting mu and dividing by sigma produces a standard normal variable with mean zero and variance one.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A random variable follows a normal distribution with mean mu and positive variance sigma squared if it has the following density on the real line:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ f_X(x)= \\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\exp\\left[-\\frac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right], \\qquad x\\in\\mathbb R. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>We write<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Its mean and variance are exactly the parameters:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\mu,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\sigma^2.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>The standard normal distribution is the special case with mean zero and variance one. I denote a standard normal variable by<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[Z\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\)<\/span>, then the standardized variable<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{Z=\\frac{X-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1).}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For an observed value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)<\/span>, the corresponding z-score is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma},\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>which measures how many standard deviations <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)<\/span> lies from the mean.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>The CLT is a principal reason Gaussian sampling distributions are ubiquitous: sums and averages of many independent contributions often become approximately normal even when the individual observations are not normal.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\sim\\mathcal{N}(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\mu, \\qquad \\mathbb{E}[X^2]=\\mu^2+\\sigma^2.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Equating with sample raw moments gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\mu_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=\\bar X,}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.8)<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat\\sigma^2_{\\mathrm{MoM}} =\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nX_i^2-\\bar X^2 =\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i-\\bar X)^2. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.9)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>To derive the maximum likelihood estimates of the mean and variance, I multiply the densities of the iid observations. The resulting likelihood is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(\\mu,\\sigma^2) =(2\\pi\\sigma^2)^{-n/2} \\exp\\left[-\\frac1{2\\sigma^2}\\sum_i(x_i-\\mu)^2\\right].\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.10)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Therefore<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\ell(\\mu,\\sigma^2) =-\\frac n2\\log(2\\pi)-\\frac n2\\log\\sigma^2 -\\frac1{2\\sigma^2}\\sum_i(x_i-\\mu)^2.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.11)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>I first differentiate the log-likelihood with respect to mu and set the derivative to zero:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\frac{\\partial\\ell}{\\partial\\mu} =\\frac1{\\sigma^2}\\sum_i(x_i-\\mu)=0,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.12)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>so<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\mu_{\\mathrm{MLE}}=\\bar X.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.13)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Substituting this and differentiating with respect to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma^2\\)<\/span> gives<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[-\\frac n{2\\sigma^2} +\\frac1{2\\sigma^4}\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar X)^2=0,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.14)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>so<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\hat\\sigma^2_{\\mathrm{MLE}} =\\frac1n\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar X)^2. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.15)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For a sample with positive residual sum of squares, this is the attained MLE. If all observations coincide, the likelihood is unbounded as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma^2\\downarrow0\\)<\/span> and there is no MLE in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma^2&gt;0\\)<\/span>.<\/p>\n<p>This MLE of variance is biased downward. The familiar unbiased sample variance is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ S^2=\\frac1{n-1}\\sum_i(X_i-\\bar X)^2. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.7.16)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This difference in denominators illustrates that maximum likelihood and unbiased estimation have different objectives: maximizing likelihood does not guarantee finite-sample unbiasedness.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.7.1)<\/strong> The density at x is one over the product of sigma and the square root of two pi, multiplied by an exponential. The exponent is the negative of the squared difference between x and mu, divided by two times sigma squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.2)<\/strong> X is normally distributed with mean mu and variance sigma squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.3)<\/strong> The expected value of X equals mu, and its variance equals sigma squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.4)<\/strong> Z follows a standard normal distribution: mean zero and variance one.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.5)<\/strong> Z equals X minus mu, all divided by sigma, and follows a standard normal distribution.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.6)<\/strong> The z-score is x minus mu, all divided by sigma.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.7)<\/strong> The expected value of X is mu, and the expected value of X squared is mu squared plus sigma squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.8)<\/strong> The method-of-moments estimate of mu equals X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.9)<\/strong> The method-of-moments estimate of mu is X bar. The estimate of sigma squared is one over n times the sum of the squared deviations from X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.10)<\/strong> The likelihood equals the quantity two pi sigma squared raised to the power negative n over two, multiplied by an exponential. The exponent is negative one over two sigma squared times the sum of the squared deviations from mu.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.11)<\/strong> The log-likelihood is negative n over two times log of two pi, minus n over two times log of sigma squared, minus the sum of squared deviations from mu divided by two sigma squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.12)<\/strong> The partial derivative of ell with respect to mu equals one over sigma squared times the sum of x sub i minus mu. I set that derivative equal to zero.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.13)<\/strong> Mu hat equals X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.14)<\/strong> Negative n divided by two sigma squared, plus the sum of squared deviations from x bar divided by two sigma to the fourth power, equals zero.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.15)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate of sigma squared is one over n times the sum, from i equals one to n, of the quantity x sub i minus x bar, squared.<\/p><p><strong>(3.7.16)<\/strong> S squared is the sum, from i equals one to n, of the squared difference between X sub i and X bar, all divided by the quantity n minus one.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-08", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.8 · Uniform Distribution", "Question": "What is a uniform distribution?", "Short": "<p>A uniform distribution on an interval assigns equal probability to subintervals of equal length. Its density is constant at one divided by the interval length inside the interval and zero outside it.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>A random variable is uniformly distributed on the interval from a to b when its density is constant there and zero elsewhere. Assuming a is less than b, the density must integrate to one, so it is<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ f_X(x)=\\frac1{b-a}\\mathbf 1_{[a,b]}(x). }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>We write<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[X\\sim\\operatorname{Uniform}(a,b).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Symmetry places its mean at the midpoint of the interval, and integration gives its variance. The resulting expressions are<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2},} \\qquad \\boxed{\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\frac{(b-a)^2}{12}.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For an estimation example, I consider independent observations uniformly distributed from zero to an unknown positive endpoint theta. The goal is to estimate that endpoint.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"method-of-moments.\">Method of Moments.<\/h4>\n<p>Since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{E}[X]=\\theta/2\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\theta_{\\mathrm{MoM}}=2\\bar X.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"maximum-likelihood-estimation.\">Maximum Likelihood Estimation.<\/h4>\n<p>To construct the likelihood, I must keep the support condition: any candidate endpoint smaller than an observed value makes the data impossible. Thus<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[L(\\theta) =\\prod_{i=1}^n\\frac1\\theta\\mathbf 1_{[0,\\theta]}(x_i) =\\theta^{-n}\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\theta\\ge x_{(n)}\\}},\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>where<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[x_{(n)}=\\max_i x_i.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.6)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\theta\\ge x_{(n)}\\)<\/span>, the factor <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\theta^{-n}\\)<\/span> decreases as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\theta\\)<\/span> grows. Therefore the maximum occurs at the smallest admissible value:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{\\hat\\theta_{\\mathrm{MLE}}=X_{(n)}=\\max_iX_i.}\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.8.7)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This is an instructive example where MLE is obtained from the boundary of the parameter space rather than by setting a derivative to zero.<\/p>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.8.1)<\/strong> The density is one divided by b minus a for x in the interval from a to b, and zero outside that interval.<\/p><p><strong>(3.8.2)<\/strong> X is uniformly distributed on the interval from a to b.<\/p><p><strong>(3.8.3)<\/strong> The mean is a plus b, all divided by two. The variance is the squared difference between b and a, divided by twelve.<\/p><p><strong>(3.8.4)<\/strong> The method-of-moments estimate theta hat equals two times X bar.<\/p><p><strong>(3.8.5)<\/strong> The likelihood is the product of one over theta times the indicator that each observation lies between zero and theta. It equals theta to the power negative n if theta is at least the sample maximum, and zero otherwise.<\/p><p><strong>(3.8.6)<\/strong> Lowercase x sub parenthesized n denotes the largest observed value, the sample maximum.<\/p><p><strong>(3.8.7)<\/strong> The maximum likelihood estimate theta hat equals the largest observation in the sample.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-09", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.9 · Student's t Distribution as a Reference Distribution", "Question": "What is Student’s t distribution?", "Short": "<p>Student’s t distribution is the distribution of a standard normal variable divided by the square root of an independent chi-squared variable divided by its degrees of freedom. It has heavier tails than a standard normal distribution and is the reference distribution for a standardized Gaussian sample mean when variance is estimated.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>Student’s t distribution accounts for extra uncertainty introduced by estimating a variance. One construction starts with a standard normal variable Z and an independent chi-squared variable U with nu degrees of freedom:<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[Z\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1),\\qquad U\\sim\\chi^2_\\nu,\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.9.1)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Using these independent variables, I form the ratio<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ T=\\frac{Z}{\\sqrt{U/\\nu}} }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.9.2)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This ratio has Student’s t distribution with nu degrees of freedom, which I write as<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[T\\sim t_\\nu.\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.9.3)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>Its density is symmetric around zero and has heavier tails than the standard normal. As <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\nu\\to\\infty\\)<\/span>,<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[t_\\nu\\xrightarrow{d}\\mathcal{N}(0,1).\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.9.4)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This construction applies directly to an iid Gaussian sample with an unknown standard deviation. Using the sample standard deviation S in the denominator gives the pivot<\/p>\n<div class=\"equation\"><p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\\boxed{ \\frac{\\bar X-\\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\\sim t_{n-1}. }\\]<\/span><\/p>\n<div class=\"eq-number\">(3.9.5)<\/div><\/div>\n<p>This pivot is the basis of the classical confidence interval and t-test for a Gaussian mean with unknown variance.<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"clarification\" class=\"unnumbered\">Clarification<\/h3>\n<p>The <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t\\)<\/span> distribution is included here primarily as a reference distribution for inference, not as a core data-generating family whose parameter MLE is typically derived in a basic statistics course.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n", "Readings": "<p><strong>(3.9.1)<\/strong> Z is standard normal, and U follows a chi-squared distribution with nu degrees of freedom. Z and U are independent.<\/p><p><strong>(3.9.2)<\/strong> T equals Z divided by the square root of the quantity U divided by nu.<\/p><p><strong>(3.9.3)<\/strong> T follows Student’s t distribution with nu degrees of freedom.<\/p><p><strong>(3.9.4)<\/strong> As nu tends to infinity, the t distribution approaches the standard normal distribution.<\/p><p><strong>(3.9.5)<\/strong> X bar minus mu, divided by the quantity S over the square root of n, follows a t distribution with n minus one degrees of freedom.<\/p>"}, {"ID": "s3-10", "Deck": "03", "Topic": "3.10 · Distribution Relationships to Remember", "Question": "What relationships connect the main probability distributions?", "Short": "<p>The distribution relationships connect binary trials, counts, waiting times, and sampling distributions. A binomial variable sums Bernoulli trials, a geometric variable counts trials to the first success, and a Poisson count arises in a rare-event limit. Exponential waiting times belong to a Poisson process, while normal and t distributions describe important standardized quantities.<\/p>\n", "Long": "<p>For a quick comparison, I summarize the construction behind each relationship in the following table.<\/p>\n<div class=\"center\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Relationship<\/strong><\/th>\n<th style=\"text-align: left;\"><strong>Meaning<\/strong><\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bernoulli <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> Binomial<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">A binomial count is the sum of iid Bernoulli trials.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Binomial <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> Poisson<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Rare-event limit: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\to\\infty\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_n\\to0\\)<\/span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(np_n\\to\\lambda\\)<\/span>.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Bernoulli <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> Geometric<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Geometric is the number of Bernoulli trials until the first success.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Poisson process <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> Exponential<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Poisson counts events; exponential describes inter-arrival waiting times.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Arbitrary iid data <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> Gaussian average<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Under finite variance, the CLT gives an asymptotically normal standardized sum/mean.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Continuous <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)<\/span> <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> Uniform<\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(F_X(X)\\sim\\operatorname{Uniform}(0,1)\\)<\/span> under the probability integral transform.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Normal sample, unknown <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\)<\/span> <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\to\\)<\/span> <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t\\)<\/span><\/td>\n<td style=\"text-align: left;\">Standardizing the sample mean by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\)<\/span> produces a Student <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t\\)<\/span> statistic.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<\/div>\n", "Readings": ""}]}}};
